# SG Publication - environment
#
# The site runs WITHOUT any of these set. Leads are logged to the console in
# development and the API route returns success so forms still work locally.

# Postgres connection string. Leave unset for local development: the site runs
# without it and logs each lead instead of storing it.
#
# ON VERCEL, use the POOLED connection string, the host with "-pooler" in it,
# and add the two query parameters. Vercel runs each request in its own
# short lived instance, so without a pooler a busy minute opens more
# connections than the database allows; and Prisma needs telling it is talking
# to one, or it prepares statements the pooler cannot keep.
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:pw@ep-xxx-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/dbname?sslmode=require&pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1"
#
# DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED is the same database WITHOUT "-pooler", and is used only
# by prisma migrate. Migrations issue DDL and take advisory locks, neither of
# which survives a transaction mode pooler, so they have to bypass it. Leave it
# unset against a plain Postgres and Prisma falls back to DATABASE_URL.
# Both want a connect_timeout, and on Neon's free tier the direct one needs a
# generous one: Neon suspends the compute after a few minutes idle and the
# first connection waits for it to wake. Without it, prisma migrate deploy
# fails with P1001 "can't reach database server", which looks like a wrong
# host and is not.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/sg_publication?schema=public"
DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=""

# Canonical site origin, used for absolute URLs in metadata, sitemap and OG images.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://sgpublication.com"

# Google Tag Manager container id, e.g. GTM-XXXXXXX.
# Leave unset and no tag manager script is injected.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GTM_ID=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The dashboard at /admin
#
# BOTH of these must be set or /admin serves a 503 and there is no way in.
# That is deliberate: everything else here degrades to still working, but a
# dashboard that opened for anyone when misconfigured would expose every
# enquiry the site has ever taken.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD is the one you type. Make it long and random, not memorable.
# ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET signs the session cookie and is never typed anywhere.
# Generate each with: node -e "console.log(crypto.randomUUID())"
# Changing the secret signs everyone out, which is how you revoke a session.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=""
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The notification email
#
# One email per enquiry, to you. Nothing is ever sent to the author: the on
# page confirmation is what tells them it worked, and answering an enquiry is
# a thing a person does by hand.
#
# With no RESEND_API_KEY the site still stores every lead and simply skips the
# send with a logged warning.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY is the only one of the three that has to be set. It comes from
# resend.com and starts re_. Without it every send is skipped and logged, and
# the forms behave exactly as they do now.
#
# MAIL_FROM must be an address on a domain VERIFIED IN RESEND, or Resend
# refuses the send with a 403. Add SPF and DKIM records for that domain in
# Resend's domain settings first; without them the mail is delivered to spam.
# Your MX records are untouched, so the mailbox keeps working as it does now.
# LEAD_NOTIFY_TO is where new enquiries land: contact@sgpublication.com, the
# same address the contact page and footer show. Leaving it blank resolves to
# the identical address through the site.email default in lib/site.ts; it is
# spelled out below so the recipient is readable without opening the code.
# Any address works here. Unlike MAIL_FROM, a recipient needs no verification.
#
# Verifying the domain in Resend lets the site SEND as contact@sgpublication.com
# and that part is done. RECEIVING at that address is a separate thing and is
# about the root MX record: it has to point at a mailbox host, such as Google
# Workspace, Microsoft 365 or Zoho, or the notification is sent and then has
# nowhere to land. Resend verification does not create the mailbox.
# There are two transports and SMTP wins when it is configured, so pick one.
#
#   SMTP sends from a mailbox you own. No API, no domain verification, and the
#   notification arrives in an inbox you already read. Use this one unless you
#   have a reason not to. Leave RESEND_API_KEY blank.
#
#   Resend posts over HTTPS instead, which survives a host that blocks
#   outbound port 587, and needs the sending domain verified first. Leave the
#   three SMTP values blank to use it.
#
# SMTP_PASS is an APP PASSWORD, not your account password. Both Gmail and
# Zoho reject the account one outright, and an app password can be revoked on
# its own if this server is ever compromised.
#
#   Namecheap Private Email    mail.privateemail.com  587   <- what this site uses
#     SMTP_USER is the full address, SMTP_PASS the mailbox password or an
#     application password. Needs the MX records below pointing at Namecheap
#     or the mail is sent and then has nowhere to arrive.
#   Gmail / Google Workspace   smtp.gmail.com         587
#     App password at myaccount.google.com/apppasswords, which requires
#     2 Step Verification to be on first. SMTP_USER is the full address.
#   Zoho Mail                  smtp.zoho.com          587
#   Microsoft 365              smtp.office365.com     587
#
# Port 465 is also fine and is treated as implicit TLS; anything else uses
# STARTTLS and the upgrade is mandatory, so a password is never sent in clear.
#
# MAIL_FROM on SMTP must be an address that mailbox is allowed to send as,
# which usually means SMTP_USER itself or a configured alias. Gmail silently
# rewrites anything else. Leave it blank and SMTP_USER is used, which is
# always allowed.
#
# The DNS half, which no amount of configuration here can substitute for. For
# Namecheap Private Email the domain needs, at the DNS host that actually
# serves the zone (Vercel, for this site, not Namecheap):
#
#   MX   @                        mx1.privateemail.com   priority 10
#   MX   @                        mx2.privateemail.com   priority 10
#   TXT  @                        v=spf1 include:spf.privateemail.com ~all
#   TXT  privateemail._domainkey  the DKIM value from Namecheap's panel
#
# Sending works without the MX records. Receiving does not, and a notification
# addressed to a mailbox the MX records do not point at is accepted and then
# lost, which looks exactly like mail that was never sent.
SMTP_HOST="mail.privateemail.com"
SMTP_PORT="587"
SMTP_USER="contact@sgpublication.com"
SMTP_PASS=""

RESEND_API_KEY=""
MAIL_FROM="SG Publication <contact@sgpublication.com>"
LEAD_NOTIFY_TO="contact@sgpublication.com"
